**Describe the bug**

The documentation is misleading. It reads :  "... it is possible for more than one to be fulfilled when k_poll() returns, ..."
But the example is not implemented accordingly:
```C
void do_stuff(void)
{
    for(;;) {
        rc = k_poll(events, ARRAY_SIZE(events), K_FOREVER);
        if (events[0].state == K_POLL_STATE_SEM_AVAILABLE) {
            k_sem_take(events[0].sem, 0);
        } else if (events[1].state == K_POLL_STATE_FIFO_DATA_AVAILABLE) {
            data = k_fifo_get(events[1].fifo, 0);
            // handle data
        } else if (events[2].state == K_POLL_STATE_MSGQ_DATA_AVAILABLE) {
            ret = k_msgq_get(events[2].msgq, buf, K_NO_WAIT);
            // handle data
        } else if (events[3].state == K_POLL_STATE_PIPE_DATA_AVAILABLE) {
            ret = k_pipe_get(events[3].pipe, buf, bytes_to_read, &bytes_read, min_xfer, K_NO_WAIT);
            // handle data
        }
        events[0].state = K_POLL_STATE_NOT_READY;
        events[1].state = K_POLL_STATE_NOT_READY;
        events[2].state = K_POLL_STATE_NOT_READY;
        events[3].state = K_POLL_STATE_NOT_READY;
    }
}
```

The `if`-`else`-chain encodes the assumption, that only one event can be active at a time and then all states are reset. If an event lower in the if else chain is active at the same time with another, then it is never handled and the state reset nonetheless.

Suggestion: Adjust ALL examples accordingly and add a note, why it is not an `if`-`else`-chain:

```C
void do_stuff(void)
{
    for(;;) {
        rc = k_poll(events, ARRAY_SIZE(events), K_FOREVER);
        if (events[0].state == K_POLL_STATE_SEM_AVAILABLE) {
            k_sem_take(events[0].sem, 0);
        }
        if (events[1].state == K_POLL_STATE_FIFO_DATA_AVAILABLE) {
            data = k_fifo_get(events[1].fifo, 0);
            // handle data
        }
        if (events[2].state == K_POLL_STATE_MSGQ_DATA_AVAILABLE) {
            ret = k_msgq_get(events[2].msgq, buf, K_NO_WAIT);
            // handle data
        }
        if (events[3].state == K_POLL_STATE_PIPE_DATA_AVAILABLE) {
            ret = k_pipe_get(events[3].pipe, buf, bytes_to_read, &bytes_read, min_xfer, K_NO_WAIT);
            // handle data
        }
        events[0].state = K_POLL_STATE_NOT_READY;
        events[1].state = K_POLL_STATE_NOT_READY;
        events[2].state = K_POLL_STATE_NOT_READY;
        events[3].state = K_POLL_STATE_NOT_READY;
    }
}
```

**Environment**

* Page: `kernel/services/polling`
* Version: main
* SHA-1: 0e830b3f100d1a1e768857450ada8be0db5a7634

**Describe the bug**
I have recently encountered an out-of-the-box issue with the blue pill board (stm32_min_dev_blue), the qdec sensor (drivers/sensors/st/qdec_stm32) and remapping timer2 pins (to use PA15 and PB3 instead of the default PA0 and PA1). If I am following the manuals correctly, this is not limited to the blue pill board, but applies to boards that use the stm32f1xx family of ARM processors.

With this setup stm32f1xx processor needs the following additional line to have the qdec work.

AFIO->MAPR |= AFIO_MAPR_SWJ_CFG_JTAGDISABLE; // JTAG-DP Disabled, SW-DP Enabled

(Solution to my problem was found on stackoverflow at ... https://stackoverflow.com/questions/45066313/issues-with-timer2-on-stm32f103-in-encoder-interface-mode)

Please also mention any information which could help others to understand
the problem you're facing:
 - What target platform are you using?
 - What have you tried to diagnose or workaround this issue?
 - Is this a regression? If yes, have you been able to "git bisect" it to a
   specific commit?
 - ...

**To Reproduce**
1. Create a project to use the stm32_min_dev_blue board.
Ensure its board/stm32_min_dev_blue.overlay contains ...

```
/ {
    chosen {
        zephyr,console = &usart3;
        zephyr,timer,qdec = &qdec2;
        zephyr,gpiob = &gpiob;
    };
};

&timers2 {
    status = "okay";
    st,prescaler = <0>;

    qdec2: qdec {
        status = "okay";
        compatible = "st,stm32-qdec";
        pinctrl-0 = <&tim2_ch1_remap3_pwm_in_pa15 &tim2_ch2_remap3_pwm_in_pb3>;
        pinctrl-names = "default";
        st,counts-per-revolution = <360>;
        st,encoder-mode = <1>;
    };
};
```

2. Ensure the prj.conf enables at least ...
```
CONFIG_GPIO=y
CONFIG_SENSOR=y
CONFIG_QDEC_STM32=y.

```

3. Wire PA15 and PB3 to two GPIOB pins that the software will control to simulate a QDEC signal.

4. Simulate the QDEC in signal in software and display the angle using ...
```
    while(1) {
       switch(iterations & 3) {
        case 0:
            gpio_pin_set(gpiob_dev, PIN_PBy, 1);
            break;
        case 1:
            gpio_pin_set(gpiob_dev, PIN_PBx, 1);
            break;
        case 2:
            gpio_pin_set(gpiob_dev, PIN_PBy, 0);
            break;
        case 3:
            gpio_pin_set(gpiob_dev, PIN_PBx, 0);
            break;
        }
        iterations++;

        sensor_sample_fetch(qdec_dev);
        sensor_channel_get(qdec_dev, SENSOR_CHAN_ROTATION, &val);
        printk("QDEC rotation angle is %d\n", val.val1);
        k_sleep(K_MSEC(500));

    }
```

5. Build and flash the project ...
```
    west build -p -b stm32_min_dev_blue <path to project>
    west flash
```

6. Observe the output.

**Expected behavior**

The output should be cycling through the numbers 0..359 (either incrementing or decrementing). However, this will not occur unless the following line is added for this configuration ...

`AFIO->MAPR |= AFIO_MAPR_SWJ_CFG_JTAGDISABLE; // JTAG-DP Disabled, SW-DP Enabled
`
**Impact**

Discovering the solution to this problem has been a pain in the proverbial backside. I expect that I will not be the only Zephyr user to be hit with this. It is also likely that other devices might be hit by this as spi1 can also remap pins PA15 and PB3.

**Environment (please complete the following information):**
  - OS : Ubuntu
  - Toolchain: Zephyr SDK
 - Zephyr version: 3.6 (older versions too)

**Solution I would like to see**

This issue is specific to timer2 (and possibly spi1) when it remaps the pins to use PA15 and PB3. Ideally, I would either like to see the initialization code detect that the remapped pins are in effect and disable the JTAG as shown above so that the use of the remapped timer2 pins are as seamless/smooth as possible. Alternatively, an optional field could be added to the device tree that indicates that the JTAG should be disabled (documenting when to use this optional field would be a 'must' if this approach is taken).

